/**
 * Arabic typography guard — `:lang(ar)` tracking neutralization.
 *
 * WHY. Arabic is a cursive script: letters JOIN, and the join is drawn by the
 * glyph itself. `letter-spacing` inserts space between glyph boxes, which pulls
 * the joining strokes apart and renders connected text as disconnected shapes.
 * This is not a matter of taste like it is in Latin — negative tracking breaks
 * the writing system, and positive tracking breaks it worse. The correct rule
 * for Arabic is: do not track at all.
 *
 * The design system's Quiet Premium type ramp is built on negative tracking
 * (`--ds-letter-spacing-tighter: -0.05em` down to `-0.01em`, plus per-heading
 * steps, in `foundation/base/typography.css`), first-party tenants push it
 * further (bithire ships `--ds-letter-spacing-display: -0.035em`), and the
 * modern skin consumes it on every text style. Before this file, `:lang(ar)`
 * and `[lang="ar"]` appeared ZERO times in the entire CSS corpus, so Arabic
 * rendered with the full Latin tracking ramp applied to it.
 *
 * WHICH @LAYER, AND WHY IT CANNOT BE ACCIDENTALLY OVERRIDDEN.
 * This file is reached through `foundation/responsive/index.css`, which both
 * public entrypoints import as `layer(rottay-responsive)` — the LAST
 * design-system layer in the canonical order:
 *
 *   theme, base, rottay-framework, rottay-reset, rottay-tokens, rottay-motion,
 *   rottay-components, rottay-engines, rottay-personality,
 *   rottay-responsive, components, utilities
 *
 * Layer precedence outranks specificity, so nothing LAYERED inside the design
 * system can beat these rules at any selector weight: not the base token scale
 * (`rottay-tokens`), not a component or engine skin, not the personality layer.
 * A guard placed with the tokens it corrects would lose to every one of those.
 * `rottay-responsive` is the right home in kind as well as in rank: it is the
 * layer for adjustments conditioned on the rendering environment, and the
 * rendering language is one of those conditions.
 *
 * KNOWN GAP — TENANT PAINT OUTRANKS THIS GUARD (open, R1 finding 2026-07-26).
 * Tenant artifacts are UNLAYERED by law (see cascade-layers:
 * TENANT_PAINT_IS_UNLAYERED), and an unlayered rule beats every layered rule
 * regardless of layer order. An earlier version of this note claimed a tenant
 * artifact could not override the guard because it lived in `rottay-tenants`;
 * that layer never existed in any shipped bundle. Four tracking tokens are
 * declared by BOTH this guard and the bithire artifact —
 * `--ds-letter-spacing-{display,heading,body,mono}` — and the artifact wins all
 * four when `lang="ar"` is on the ROOT element.
 *
 * Nested Arabic is unaffected: custom properties resolve from the nearest
 * declaring ancestor, so a `<div lang="ar">` inside a Latin page still gets 0.
 * Only a wholly-Arabic document is exposed.
 *
 * FIXED 2026-07-26 by `language-arabic-root.css`: an UNLAYERED
 * `html[lang]:lang(ar)` rule at (0,2,1), which outranks the artifact's (0,1,1)
 * without `!important` (that would also defeat legitimate application-owned
 * Arabic typography). This file keeps the layered guard, which is still what
 * neutralizes every LAYERED emitter and the nested-subtree case.
 *
 * What CAN still override it is deliberate and application-owned: a consumer's
 * own `components`/`utilities` layer, unlayered application CSS, `!important`,
 * or an inline `style`. An application that genuinely wants tracked Arabic has
 * to say so explicitly.
 *
 * NO CLASSIC/RUSTIC EDIT REQUIRED. The guard is engine-agnostic: it corrects
 * the tokens all three engines read and the `letter-spacing` property all three
 * paint, so classic (Ant Design) and rustic inherit it untouched.
 *
 * `:lang(ar)` rather than `[lang="ar"]`: it matches `ar`, `ar-SA`, `ar-EG` and
 * every other regional variant, and it matches through inheritance, so tagging
 * the document or a single Arabic subtree both work.
 *
 * SCOPE NOTE. `letter-spacing` and custom properties both inherit, so a Latin
 * ISLAND inside an Arabic document (`<span lang="en">` under `<html lang="ar">`)
 * inherits neutral tracking. That is a deliberate trade: the alternative is a
 * second copy of the whole tracking scale that would silently drift from the
 * canonical one. An island that needs its tracking back declares it, which is
 * a bounded, local act — and correcting an island's tracking is cosmetic,
 * whereas breaking Arabic joining is not.
 */

/* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
   1. Token neutralization.

   The floor in section 2 covers everything painted from a stylesheet. This
   section covers what it cannot reach: components that resolve a tracking
   token in an INLINE style (e.g. `letterSpacing: 'var(--ds-letter-spacing-
   heading)'` in the modern cockpit/workbench/page-shell headers). An inline
   style outranks every layer, so the only way to correct it is to change the
   value the `var()` resolves to — which is what re-declaring the token on the
   Arabic subtree does.

   Values are `0` rather than `normal` so a token that lands inside a `calc()`
   stays valid; for tracking the two are visually identical.
   --------------------------------------------------------------------------- */
:lang(ar) {
  /* Canonical tracking scale (foundation/base/typography.css). */
  --ds-letter-spacing-tighter: 0;
  --ds-letter-spacing-tight: 0;
  --ds-letter-spacing-snug: 0;
  --ds-letter-spacing-subtle: 0;
  --ds-letter-spacing-subtle-wide: 0;
  --ds-letter-spacing-wide: 0;
  --ds-letter-spacing-wider: 0;
  --ds-letter-spacing-widest: 0;

  /* Per-heading tracking steps. */
  --ds-letter-spacing-h1: 0;
  --ds-letter-spacing-h2: 0;
  --ds-letter-spacing-h3: 0;
  --ds-letter-spacing-h4: 0;
  --ds-letter-spacing-h5: 0;
  --ds-letter-spacing-h6: 0;

  /* Brand/tenant tracking channels — the ones first-party artifacts push
     negative, and the ones BrandTheme exposes to customers. */
  --ds-letter-spacing-display: 0;
  --ds-letter-spacing-heading: 0;
  --ds-letter-spacing-body: 0;
  --ds-letter-spacing-mono: 0;
  --ds-personality-typography-heading-letter-spacing: 0;
  --ds-typography-display-letter-spacing: 0;
  --ds-typography-heading-letter-spacing: 0;
  --ds-typography-body-letter-spacing: 0;

  /* Semantic type roles consumed by the modern skin's `[data-text-style]`
     rules and by the Text/Heading primitives. */
  --ds-type-display-letter-spacing: 0;
  --ds-type-page-title-letter-spacing: 0;
  --ds-type-section-title-letter-spacing: 0;
  --ds-type-body-letter-spacing: 0;
  --ds-type-supporting-letter-spacing: 0;
  --ds-type-label-letter-spacing: 0;
  --ds-type-caption-letter-spacing: 0;
  --ds-type-code-letter-spacing: 0;
  --ds-type-numeric-letter-spacing: 0;
}

/* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
   2. Property floor.

   The complete guard for stylesheet-declared tracking, including the literal
   values hard-coded in skins (widget-board, record-facts, markdown-view,
   surface-section-card, decision-*, oauth-transition) that ride no token at
   all, and including tracking tokens added after this file was written. It
   needs no specificity arms race: `rottay-responsive` outranks every layer
   those declarations live in.

   `normal` rather than `0` so the browser keeps its justification-aware
   default spacing behavior for the property itself.
   --------------------------------------------------------------------------- */
:lang(ar) {
  letter-spacing: normal;
}


}

/* Arabic tracking floor at ROOT scope, deliberately UNLAYERED: the tenant
   artifact is unlayered too, so a layered guard can never reach it. See
   foundation/responsive/language-arabic-root.css. */
/**
 * Arabic tracking guard — ROOT scope, unlayered.
 *
 * WHY A SECOND FILE. `foundation/responsive/language-arabic.css` carries the
 * same neutralization inside `@layer rottay-responsive`, which is correct for
 * every LAYERED emitter. It is not enough for the one emitter that is not
 * layered: the tenant artifact. Tenant paint is unlayered by law
 * (cascade-layers: TENANT_PAINT_IS_UNLAYERED) and therefore outranks every
 * layer regardless of specificity, so a tenant that ships negative display
 * tracking -- bithire ships `--ds-letter-spacing-display: -0.035em` -- won it
 * back on a wholly-Arabic document and broke the joining of the script.
 *
 * WHY NOT `!important`. It would also defeat an application that legitimately
 * sets Arabic typography for its own surfaces, and it would put a correctness
 * floor and a brand preference into the same, unarguable bucket. Specificity is
 * enough and stays negotiable: `html[lang]:lang(ar)` is (0,2,1), which beats
 * the artifact's `:is(html[data-tenant='x'], …)` at (0,1,1) while remaining
 * reachable by any app rule that genuinely means to override it.
 *
 * WHY ONLY THE ROOT. Custom properties resolve from the NEAREST declaring
 * ancestor, so a `<div lang="ar">` inside a Latin document already wins over an
 * `html`-scoped artifact declaration by proximity — no specificity contest
 * occurs. Only the case where `lang` sits on `<html>` itself is contested, and
 * that is exactly what this file addresses. `:lang()` matches regional variants,
 * so `ar`, `ar-SA` and `ar-EG` are all covered.
